  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 233 PM EDT Mon Jul 27 2026\n\nAfternoon GOES satellite imagery reveals mostly clear skies overhead \nUpper Michigan with increased subsidence and dry air advection \nbehind this morning's round of thunderstorms. A few remaining \nscattered showers are noted on the Thunder Bay, Canada radar \napproaching Isle Royale, but overall thinking strong thunderstorm \npotential is very limited as these showers/storms cross the island \nand work towards the Keweenaw. Thus, the remainder of the evening \nshould remain largely precip free across the UP. Also noted on \nsatellite imagery is the presence of transient marine fog across \nmuch of Lake Superior. With an uptick in northerly winds expected, \nmarine fog may hug the shorelines and Keweenaw Peninsula, though \nconfidence is low on if it will penetrate inland anywhere overnight. \nPartly cloudy skies should allow low temperatures to fall into the \n50s to low 60s tonight, warmest near the lakeshores.\n\nQuieter weather can be expected headed into the rest of the week as \nthe seemingly endless Plains ridge churns and geopotential heights \nbuild into the Upper Midwest / Great Lakes. Seasonably warm temps in \nthe mid to low 80s will be the rule through at least Friday. A \nflattening of the ridge and more zonal flow aloft later this week \n(Thursday-Friday timeframe) brings returning PoPs to the forecast, \nbut for now enjoy some classic summer days ahead.",
